    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1951-p42.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The repeating tail gives \\(x=\\sqrt{1+x},\\) so \\[ x^2-x-1=0. \\] Since \\(x\\ge0,\\) \\(x=\\frac{1+\\sqrt5}{2},\\) which lies strictly between \\(1\\) and \\(2.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "The expression under the first radical contains another copy of \\(x\\)",
        "Solve \\(x=\\sqrt{1+x}\\) and keep the nonnegative root"
      ],
